  "articleBody": "Texas weather can be pretty wild, right? Those intense sun rays, strong winds, and sudden hailstorms can really do a number on your roof, often in ways you can't spot from the ground. It’s super important to get a professional roof health assessment after any significant weather event, even if you don't see obvious damage. Think of it like a check-up after a tough workout – you want to make sure everything's still working right.\n\n### What Can Change the Right Choice\nChoosing when to get an assessment really comes down to recent weather and your roof's age. If you've just had a big hailstorm, or if your roof is getting up there in years, a check-up is a no-brainer. Also, consider any past repairs or known issues. Sometimes, a tiny bit of damage from a new storm can make an old problem much worse. A qualified professional can identify issues like displaced shingles, compromised flashing, or granule loss that might seem minor but can lead to big headaches down the road. They'll also check for things like attic moisture, which is a huge red flag.\n\n### What to Expect During the Process\nWhen we do a roof health assessment, we're not just glancing up from the driveway. Our team reviews your roof's history, materials, and how it's been exposed to weather. We then do a thorough inspection, both from the ground and directly on the roof when it's safe. We’re looking at everything: drainage, flashing, any penetrations, and the overall condition of your roofing materials. We'll even check inside your attic for moisture issues. We document all our findings with photos and a detailed report, so you know exactly what's going on. We want to protect your home and give you peace of mind that lasts for years to come.\n\n### What to Do Next\nDon't wait for a small issue to become a major repair. If you've been through a recent storm or just haven't had a roof check-up in a while, reach out to Discounted Roofing Co. for a professional roof health assessment. We're here to help protect your Texas home.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-02"
}